1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,828
Why does your cat purr?

2
00:00:01,828 --> 00:00:05,286
Ask most people and they'll say a purr just means a happy cat.

3
00:00:05,286 --> 00:00:08,569
But cats also purr at the vet and when they're badly hurt.

4
00:00:08,569 --> 00:00:11,321
So what is that purr really trying to tell you?

5
00:00:11,921 --> 00:00:14,118
We'll talk more about that in this video.

6
00:00:15,321 --> 00:00:18,485
Picture a slow Sunday morning and you're still in bed.

7
00:00:18,485 --> 00:00:21,708
The cat climbs up and plants itself right on your chest.

8
00:00:21,708 --> 00:00:24,435
A few seconds later the little motor kicks in.

9
00:00:24,435 --> 00:00:26,066
It's a soft and steady rumble

10
00:00:26,066 --> 00:00:28,302
that you feel more than you actually hear.

11
00:00:28,302 --> 00:00:31,093
Your whole ribcage seems to hum along with it.

12
00:00:31,093 --> 00:00:35,050
The cat's eyes drift shut and somehow the room gets a little quieter.

13
00:00:35,050 --> 00:00:37,699
It might be one of the nicest sounds in the world.

14
00:00:37,699 --> 00:00:41,361
It's also one of the strangest. For a very long time nobody could

15
00:00:41,361 --> 00:00:43,411
even explain how a cat makes it.

16
00:00:43,411 --> 00:00:45,168
And the reasons behind it turn out

17
00:00:45,168 --> 00:00:48,127
to be way more interesting than plain old happiness.

18
00:00:48,127 --> 00:00:50,447
So let's go right back to the start.

19
00:00:50,447 --> 00:00:53,822
Let's go back to a cardboard box full of newborn kittens.

20
00:00:53,822 --> 00:00:56,662
A kitten comes into the world blind and deaf.

21
00:00:56,662 --> 00:00:59,966
Its eyes stay sealed for roughly the first week or two.

22
00:00:59,966 --> 00:01:02,900
Its ears are folded down and closed up tight.

23
00:01:02,900 --> 00:01:05,926
It can't walk yet and it can't keep itself warm.

24
00:01:05,926 --> 00:01:09,592
About all it can manage is to wriggle toward its mother and eat.

25
00:01:09,592 --> 00:01:12,593
Now think about the problem that leaves the mother with.

26
00:01:12,593 --> 00:01:14,144
She's got a heap of tiny kittens

27
00:01:14,144 --> 00:01:16,064
that can't see her or hear her.

28
00:01:16,064 --> 00:01:19,072
How is she supposed to let them know she's close by?

29
00:01:19,072 --> 00:01:21,946
And how do they let her know they're doing alright?

30
00:01:21,946 --> 00:01:24,423
The answer turns out to be the purr.

31
00:01:24,423 --> 00:01:27,448
Kittens start purring when they're only a few days old.

32
00:01:27,448 --> 00:01:30,239
The mother purrs right back at them while they nurse.

33
00:01:30,239 --> 00:01:32,747
A deaf kitten can't hear much of anything.

34
00:01:32,747 --> 00:01:33,895
What it can do is feel

35
00:01:33,895 --> 00:01:36,811
a vibration running through its whole little body.

36
00:01:36,811 --> 00:01:40,008
So the purr becomes a quiet signal passed between them.

37
00:01:40,008 --> 00:01:42,477
The mother is saying that she's right here.

38
00:01:42,477 --> 00:01:45,400
The kittens are saying that everything is fine.

39
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:48,389
There's a clever little detail hiding in this too.

40
00:01:48,389 --> 00:01:51,148
A kitten can't meow while it's drinking milk.

41
00:01:51,148 --> 00:01:53,655
Its mouth is far too busy for that.

42
00:01:53,655 --> 00:01:56,607
But it can purr with a mouth full of milk just fine.

43
00:01:56,607 --> 00:01:59,785
So the purr lets a kitten talk to its mom while it eats.

44
00:01:59,785 --> 00:02:01,472
That means purring is one of the very

45
00:02:01,472 --> 00:02:03,541
first things a cat ever does.

46
00:02:03,541 --> 00:02:07,098
It belongs to the warmest and safest days of its whole life.

47
00:02:07,098 --> 00:02:09,978
And just like kneading it never really goes away.

48
00:02:09,978 --> 00:02:11,284
So when your grown cat purrs

49
00:02:11,284 --> 00:02:13,843
on your lap it's speaking its oldest language.

50
00:02:13,843 --> 00:02:17,112
A lot of experts think it's treating you a bit like its mother.

51
00:02:17,112 --> 00:02:20,562
You're warm and you're soft and you're the one who brings the food.

52
00:02:20,562 --> 00:02:23,626
From where the cat is sitting that's a pretty decent mom.

53
00:02:23,626 --> 00:02:26,446
But how does a cat actually make that sound?

54
00:02:26,446 --> 00:02:30,381
Honestly this one stumped scientists for a surprisingly long time.

55
00:02:30,381 --> 00:02:33,107
Some of the early guesses were a little out there.

56
00:02:33,107 --> 00:02:34,645
One old theory said the purr came

57
00:02:34,645 --> 00:02:37,527
from blood rushing through a big vein in the chest.

58
00:02:37,527 --> 00:02:41,549
Another claimed cats had a second set of vocal cords just for purring.

59
00:02:41,549 --> 00:02:43,752
Neither one held up in the end.

60
00:02:43,752 --> 00:02:46,790
For years the best answer came down to muscles.

61
00:02:46,790 --> 00:02:49,634
Tucked inside a cat's throat is the voice box.

62
00:02:49,634 --> 00:02:53,896
When a cat purrs the muscles around that voice box twitch incredibly fast.

63
00:02:53,896 --> 00:02:57,445
We're talking twenty to thirty twitches every single second.

64
00:02:57,445 --> 00:03:01,516
Each twitch snaps the gap between the vocal cords open and shut again.

65
00:03:01,516 --> 00:03:05,340
The air rushing through gets chopped up into that quick and steady rumble.

66
00:03:05,340 --> 00:03:06,490
And the cat keeps it going

67
00:03:06,490 --> 00:03:08,933
while it breathes in and while it breathes out.

68
00:03:08,933 --> 00:03:12,326
That's why a purr can roll on and on without a single break.

69
00:03:12,326 --> 00:03:15,159
Most cat sounds only happen on the way out.

70
00:03:15,159 --> 00:03:17,669
A meow is made with air going out.

71
00:03:17,669 --> 00:03:20,253
A hiss is made with air going out too.

72
00:03:20,253 --> 00:03:23,624
The purr is special because it runs in both directions.

73
00:03:23,624 --> 00:03:25,511
If you listen really closely you can

74
00:03:25,511 --> 00:03:28,355
sometimes catch it shifting as the cat breathes.

75
00:03:28,355 --> 00:03:32,482
Then in 2023 a team of scientists turned up a twist.

76
00:03:32,482 --> 00:03:36,060
They studied the voice boxes of cats that had died of illness.

77
00:03:36,060 --> 00:03:38,610
And they found a voice box could make a purring sound

78
00:03:38,610 --> 00:03:40,632
with no twitching muscles at all.

79
00:03:40,632 --> 00:03:43,662
Just blowing air through it was enough to do the trick.

80
00:03:43,662 --> 00:03:47,561
Inside the vocal cords they spotted small pads of soft tissue.

81
00:03:47,561 --> 00:03:51,382
Those pads make the cords heavier so they can wobble very slowly.

82
00:03:51,382 --> 00:03:54,811
That's how such a small animal pulls off such a deep sound.

83
00:03:54,811 --> 00:03:57,823
So the real answer is probably a bit of both.

84
00:03:57,823 --> 00:04:01,208
The brain and the muscles get the purr started and keep it going.

85
00:04:01,208 --> 00:04:03,162
The odd build of the voice box gives it

86
00:04:03,162 --> 00:04:05,128
that deep and steady rumble.

87
00:04:05,128 --> 00:04:07,858
Scientists are still sorting out the details.

88
00:04:07,858 --> 00:04:11,171
And I find it kind of amazing that one of the most familiar sounds

89
00:04:11,171 --> 00:04:13,690
in your house is still partly a mystery.

90
00:04:13,690 --> 00:04:15,849
Here's another fun one for you.

91
00:04:15,849 --> 00:04:18,102
Not every cat can purr at all.

92
00:04:18,102 --> 00:04:20,786
Your house cat purrs and so does a cheetah.

93
00:04:20,786 --> 00:04:23,542
So does a puma and a lynx and a bobcat.

94
00:04:23,542 --> 00:04:26,363
The big famous cats are a different story though.

95
00:04:26,363 --> 00:04:28,010
Lions and tigers and leopards

96
00:04:28,010 --> 00:04:30,781
and jaguars can't purr the way your cat does.

97
00:04:30,781 --> 00:04:33,221
Those four are the ones that can roar.

98
00:04:33,221 --> 00:04:35,522
For a long time people thought it all came down

99
00:04:35,522 --> 00:04:37,492
to a small bone in the throat.

100
00:04:37,492 --> 00:04:40,954
In the roaring cats part of that bone stays soft and stretchy.

101
00:04:40,954 --> 00:04:44,422
That was supposed to explain why they roar instead of purr.

102
00:04:44,422 --> 00:04:47,136
These days the picture looks a lot messier.

103
00:04:47,136 --> 00:04:50,873
The shape of their vocal cords seems to matter quite a bit too.

104
00:04:50,873 --> 00:04:54,272
A lion can make a sort of purring sound as it breathes out.

105
00:04:54,272 --> 00:04:56,304
What it can't do is keep the rumble going

106
00:04:56,304 --> 00:04:57,998
in and out like your cat.

107
00:04:57,998 --> 00:05:00,526
So there's a rough rule in the cat family.

108
00:05:00,526 --> 00:05:03,414
Most cats get to purr or roar but not both.

109
00:05:03,414 --> 00:05:06,178
Very few of them can really pull off the two.

110
00:05:06,178 --> 00:05:08,600
Your little cat ended up with the purr.

111
00:05:08,600 --> 00:05:11,917
And honestly that's the better deal when you're curled up on a lap.

112
00:05:11,917 --> 00:05:14,900
People have been charmed by this sound for ages.

113
00:05:14,900 --> 00:05:19,616
The English word purr was first written down back in the 1600s.

114
00:05:19,616 --> 00:05:22,891
It was made to sound just like the noise it describes.

115
00:05:22,891 --> 00:05:26,175
Plenty of other languages did the exact same thing.

116
00:05:26,175 --> 00:05:29,107
In French a contented cat goes ronron.

117
00:05:29,107 --> 00:05:31,678
In German a happy cat will schnurren.

118
00:05:31,678 --> 00:05:34,522
In Japanese the purr is goro goro.

119
00:05:34,522 --> 00:05:37,295
Pretty much anywhere people live with cats they came up

120
00:05:37,295 --> 00:05:38,945
with a word for that rumble.

121
00:05:38,945 --> 00:05:41,628
Okay so now we get to the real puzzle.

122
00:05:41,628 --> 00:05:44,301
If a purr means a happy cat then why do cats purr

123
00:05:44,301 --> 00:05:45,607
when they're miserable?

124
00:05:45,607 --> 00:05:48,179
Ask any vet and you'll hear the same thing.

125
00:05:48,179 --> 00:05:50,553
Cats purr up on the exam table.

126
00:05:50,553 --> 00:05:52,604
They purr when they're frightened.

127
00:05:52,604 --> 00:05:55,572
They purr when they're injured and in real pain.

128
00:05:55,572 --> 00:05:59,185
Mother cats purr while they're giving birth. And some cats purr softly

129
00:05:59,185 --> 00:06:01,557
when they're very old and close to the end.

130
00:06:01,557 --> 00:06:04,352
None of those are what you'd call happy moments.

131
00:06:04,352 --> 00:06:07,330
So the purr can't simply mean a cat is content.

132
00:06:07,330 --> 00:06:09,666
A lot of experts now see the purr as a way

133
00:06:09,666 --> 00:06:11,715
for a cat to calm itself down.

134
00:06:11,715 --> 00:06:15,302
It's a bit like a person humming under their breath when they're nervous.

135
00:06:15,302 --> 00:06:18,424
Or rocking back and forth a little when something hurts.

136
00:06:18,424 --> 00:06:21,091
That steady rhythm seems to soothe the cat.

137
00:06:21,091 --> 00:06:24,530
Some scientists think purring might even help the body release chemicals

138
00:06:24,530 --> 00:06:28,272
that ease pain and stress. Nobody has proven that one yet.

139
00:06:28,272 --> 00:06:30,576
But it would explain an awful lot.

140
00:06:30,576 --> 00:06:33,925
It fits the kitten story too. From the very first days of life

141
00:06:33,925 --> 00:06:36,062
a purr was the sound of being safe.

142
00:06:36,062 --> 00:06:37,690
So when a grown cat feels scared

143
00:06:37,690 --> 00:06:40,294
or sore it reaches back for that old sound.

144
00:06:40,294 --> 00:06:42,491
It's almost as if the cat is telling itself

145
00:06:42,491 --> 00:06:44,277
that it's going to be okay.

146
00:06:44,277 --> 00:06:46,602
And this matters for you as an owner.

147
00:06:46,602 --> 00:06:49,394
A purring cat isn't always a healthy cat.

148
00:06:49,394 --> 00:06:53,233
Cats are remarkably good at hiding pain. Out in the wild a cat

149
00:06:53,233 --> 00:06:56,018
that looks weak can quickly become somebody's dinner.

150
00:06:56,018 --> 00:06:59,206
So a sick cat might tuck itself away somewhere and purr.

151
00:06:59,206 --> 00:07:02,345
If your cat is purring but also hiding or skipping meals,

152
00:07:02,345 --> 00:07:03,984
please give your vet a call.

153
00:07:03,984 --> 00:07:07,799
Now here's what I think is the most surprising idea of the lot.

154
00:07:07,799 --> 00:07:11,223
Some scientists think the purr might actually help a cat heal.

155
00:07:11,223 --> 00:07:14,741
You've probably heard the old saying that cats have nine lives.

156
00:07:14,741 --> 00:07:18,396
And cats do seem to bounce back from injuries remarkably well.

157
00:07:18,396 --> 00:07:20,080
There are old stories among vets

158
00:07:20,080 --> 00:07:23,161
that cats mend broken bones faster than dogs do.

159
00:07:23,161 --> 00:07:25,426
Cats also seem to run into fewer problems

160
00:07:25,426 --> 00:07:28,251
with their bones and joints as they get older.

161
00:07:28,251 --> 00:07:31,856
Around the year 2001 a researcher named Elizabeth von

162
00:07:31,856 --> 00:07:35,300
Muggenthaler recorded the purrs of lots of different cats.

163
00:07:35,300 --> 00:07:39,048
She recorded house cats and cheetahs and pumas and more besides.

164
00:07:39,048 --> 00:07:40,383
Most of those purrs sat

165
00:07:40,383 --> 00:07:44,329
in a low range of about twenty five to fifty vibrations per second.

166
00:07:44,329 --> 00:07:48,022
And that happened to line up with something interesting from human medicine.

167
00:07:48,022 --> 00:07:52,690
Doctors have studied gentle vibration as a way to help bones grow stronger.

168
00:07:52,690 --> 00:07:54,898
Some of the frequencies used in that work land

169
00:07:54,898 --> 00:07:57,022
in the same low range as a purr.

170
00:07:57,022 --> 00:08:01,101
So the idea goes something like this. A cat lounging around for hours

171
00:08:01,101 --> 00:08:04,553
on end might use its purr like a built in massage machine.

172
00:08:04,553 --> 00:08:06,221
That low buzz could keep its bones

173
00:08:06,221 --> 00:08:08,794
and muscles strong while it naps the day away.

174
00:08:08,794 --> 00:08:12,299
It might even help an injury knit back together a bit faster.

175
00:08:12,299 --> 00:08:15,574
It's a lovely idea and I really want it to be true.

176
00:08:15,574 --> 00:08:18,352
But we have to be honest about where it stands.

177
00:08:18,352 --> 00:08:21,702
Nobody has actually proven that purring heals cats.

178
00:08:21,702 --> 00:08:24,697
It's a theory that happens to fit some of the facts.

179
00:08:24,697 --> 00:08:28,683
Nobody has done the big careful studies that would show it really works.

180
00:08:28,683 --> 00:08:32,589
So file it under fascinating maybe and not under settled fact.

181
00:08:32,589 --> 00:08:35,123
Some people take it one step further.

182
00:08:35,123 --> 00:08:38,314
They say a purring cat can help heal humans as well.

183
00:08:38,314 --> 00:08:41,902
That hasn't been proven either. Still plenty of cat owners will tell you

184
00:08:41,902 --> 00:08:44,498
a purring cat takes the edge off their stress.

185
00:08:44,498 --> 00:08:46,044
And one large study found

186
00:08:46,044 --> 00:08:49,250
that cat owners were less likely to die of a heart attack.

187
00:08:49,250 --> 00:08:52,467
Whether the purr is the reason is anybody's guess.

188
00:08:52,467 --> 00:08:54,897
But it's hard to argue with a warm cat rumbling

189
00:08:54,897 --> 00:08:57,019
on your chest after a rotten day.

190
00:08:57,019 --> 00:09:00,217
Now let's talk about the purr your cat saves just for you.

191
00:09:00,217 --> 00:09:01,401
Have you ever been woken up

192
00:09:01,401 --> 00:09:03,782
at five in the morning by a purring cat?

193
00:09:03,782 --> 00:09:05,521
One that parks itself on your pillow

194
00:09:05,521 --> 00:09:07,514
and purrs straight into your face?

195
00:09:07,514 --> 00:09:10,986
There's a reason that particular purr is so hard to ignore.

196
00:09:10,986 --> 00:09:14,140
In 2009 a scientist named Karen McComb

197
00:09:14,140 --> 00:09:17,666
at the University of Sussex decided to dig into this.

198
00:09:17,666 --> 00:09:21,178
Her own cat had a habit of waking her up early for breakfast.

199
00:09:21,178 --> 00:09:25,385
So she and her team recorded cats purring in two different situations.

200
00:09:25,385 --> 00:09:28,286
In the first one the cats were just relaxing.

201
00:09:28,286 --> 00:09:31,742
In the second one the cats were asking their owners for food.

202
00:09:31,742 --> 00:09:34,513
Then they played both kinds of purr back to people.

203
00:09:34,513 --> 00:09:37,976
Even folks who had never owned a cat could tell them apart.

204
00:09:37,976 --> 00:09:41,444
The food purrs came across as more urgent and less pleasant.

205
00:09:41,444 --> 00:09:44,308
People found them a whole lot harder to tune out.

206
00:09:44,308 --> 00:09:47,483
When the team dug a little deeper they found the secret.

207
00:09:47,483 --> 00:09:50,713
Hidden inside the hungry purr was a high pitched sound.

208
00:09:50,713 --> 00:09:53,522
It was a bit like a cry stirred into the rumble.

209
00:09:53,522 --> 00:09:54,904
And it sat in a range close

210
00:09:54,904 --> 00:09:56,909
to the cry of a human baby.

211
00:09:56,909 --> 00:09:59,919
People are wired to respond when a baby cries.

212
00:09:59,919 --> 00:10:01,222
So the cat had figured out how

213
00:10:01,222 --> 00:10:03,328
to tug on that very same string.

214
00:10:03,328 --> 00:10:06,200
Scientists call this the solicitation purr.

215
00:10:06,200 --> 00:10:09,312
Basically it's a purr with a request baked right in.

216
00:10:09,312 --> 00:10:12,968
Cats that live closely with one person seem to use it the most.

217
00:10:12,968 --> 00:10:16,587
Over time they learn exactly which sound gets the bowl filled.

218
00:10:16,587 --> 00:10:19,395
In other words your cat may well have trained you.

219
00:10:19,395 --> 00:10:21,828
And it pulled the whole thing off with a purr.

220
00:10:21,828 --> 00:10:24,204
So listen closely the next time your cat purrs

221
00:10:24,204 --> 00:10:25,558
at you near the kitchen.

222
00:10:25,558 --> 00:10:28,742
You might catch that little cry tucked inside the rumble.

223
00:10:28,742 --> 00:10:31,154
That isn't just love doing the talking.

224
00:10:31,154 --> 00:10:33,495
That's a breakfast order being placed.

225
00:10:33,495 --> 00:10:36,138
Purrs come in all sorts of sizes too.

226
00:10:36,138 --> 00:10:38,578
Some cats purr so softly you only notice

227
00:10:38,578 --> 00:10:40,606
when your fingers brush their throat.

228
00:10:40,606 --> 00:10:44,171
Others purr so loudly you can hear them from across the room.

229
00:10:44,171 --> 00:10:46,070
The loudest purr on record belonged

230
00:10:46,070 --> 00:10:48,844
to a cat named Merlin from Devon in England.

231
00:10:48,844 --> 00:10:53,584
Back in 2015 his purr was measured at almost sixty eight decibels.

232
00:10:53,584 --> 00:10:56,230
That's about as loud as two people chatting.

233
00:10:56,230 --> 00:10:59,613
It's close to the hum of a dishwasher running in the kitchen.

234
00:10:59,613 --> 00:11:02,789
And all of that came out of one ordinary house cat.

235
00:11:02,789 --> 00:11:04,878
Some cats purr while they eat.

236
00:11:04,878 --> 00:11:07,363
Some cats purr right through their naps.

237
00:11:07,363 --> 00:11:10,652
Some start up the moment you so much as glance at them.

238
00:11:10,652 --> 00:11:13,262
And some cats hardly ever purr at all.

239
00:11:13,262 --> 00:11:15,623
That's completely normal as well.

240
00:11:15,623 --> 00:11:18,215
A quiet cat isn't an unhappy cat.

241
00:11:18,215 --> 00:11:21,108
Some cats just show how they feel in other ways.

242
00:11:21,108 --> 00:11:23,774
So how can you tell what your cat's purr means?

243
00:11:23,774 --> 00:11:27,377
The trick is to watch the whole cat instead of only listening.

244
00:11:27,377 --> 00:11:30,840
A happy purr usually comes with a loose and relaxed body.

245
00:11:30,840 --> 00:11:33,936
The eyes are half closed or doing that slow blink.

246
00:11:33,936 --> 00:11:36,204
The tail lies calm and still.

247
00:11:36,204 --> 00:11:39,185
The cat might knead or rub its face against you.

248
00:11:39,185 --> 00:11:42,259
That's the purr of a cat that feels completely safe.

249
00:11:42,259 --> 00:11:44,516
A stressed purr looks pretty different.

250
00:11:44,516 --> 00:11:47,001
The body is tense or hunched up tight.

251
00:11:47,001 --> 00:11:49,309
The ears may be pulled back flat.

252
00:11:49,309 --> 00:11:52,361
The cat might be hiding or breathing a little fast.

253
00:11:52,361 --> 00:11:55,670
That purr is a cat working hard to calm itself down.

254
00:11:55,670 --> 00:11:57,178
And the hungry purr tends to come

255
00:11:57,178 --> 00:11:59,493
with a cat that is staring right at you.

256
00:11:59,493 --> 00:12:01,955
Or staring at the food bowl instead.

257
00:12:01,955 --> 00:12:04,766
Or pacing back and forth between the two of you.

258
00:12:04,766 --> 00:12:06,878
You'll learn that one fast enough.

259
00:12:06,878 --> 00:12:09,184
Trust me your cat will make sure of it.

260
00:12:09,184 --> 00:12:11,226
So why does your cat purr?

261
00:12:11,226 --> 00:12:14,345
Because it's the very first language it ever spoke.

262
00:12:14,345 --> 00:12:18,379
It started in a pile of blind kittens snuggled against a warm mother.

263
00:12:18,379 --> 00:12:21,758
Because the purr is how a cat tells you that it feels safe.

264
00:12:21,758 --> 00:12:25,483
Because it's also how a scared or hurting cat calms itself down.

265
00:12:25,483 --> 00:12:28,389
Because it might even help its bones and body heal.

266
00:12:28,389 --> 00:12:31,984
And because some clever cats have learned to use it to get breakfast.

267
00:12:31,984 --> 00:12:34,361
A purr isn't just one message.

268
00:12:34,361 --> 00:12:37,746
It's a whole bundle of messages wrapped up in one rumble.

269
00:12:37,746 --> 00:12:40,736
To read it properly you have to look at the whole cat.

270
00:12:40,736 --> 00:12:42,973
But here's the part I like best.

271
00:12:42,973 --> 00:12:46,917
Your cat once purred only for its mother in those first blind days.

272
00:12:46,917 --> 00:12:49,569
Now it purrs for you. So soak it in the next time

273
00:12:49,569 --> 00:12:52,262
that warm little motor starts up on your chest.

274
00:12:52,262 --> 00:12:54,453
That sound is the oldest way a cat knows

275
00:12:54,453 --> 00:12:56,147
to tell you that you're family.
